WHATNOW

What libtam is doing now and what comes next. Read this first; revise it
at the end of every session (work/README-work).

Updated: 2026-11-23, with W5


STATE

  Version 0.9.1 on branch dev (the VERSION file is the version's source
  of truth). Follows the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard (W3). Two static
  archives by default: libtam.a, the core, which needs only libc, and
  libtam-portal.a, which needs D-Bus. The optional libtam-display.a and
  libtam-display-session.a are experimental.

  W5 fixes external opener exec error reporting.

  Recent work: 0.7.0 added the terminal foundation (tam_uni, tam_key,
  tam_tty), used by tam-edit, tam-clam and tam-last; 0.7.1 moved the
  version into VERSION; 0.8.0 retired tam_bundle_guard and added
  tam_cultivar helpers; W2 renamed develop to dev; W3 brought the
  repository up to the tam-idol 0.2.0 standard. W4 defaulted make
  install to ~/.local. Earlier records live in work/history/.

  Plain make install now targets ~/.local and needs no root; an explicit
  PREFIX still wins. tam-last, tam-clam and tam-pane link libtam.a from
  there.


NEXT

  No libtam work is queued. These are the known requests, each to start
  only when Fred asks:

  1. Kitty keyboard protocol decoding in tam_key (CSI u, release events,
     the flags query), so our programs can ask tam-last for every key.
     tam-last's P2 lists it as an open point. tam-last's
     tests/test_keys.c already round-trips its legacy encoding through
     tam_key and lists what the legacy encoding loses.

  2. A Wayland wire module and evdev keymaps, shared by tam-pane (server
     side, its P6) and tam-last (client side, in src/wl/ and src/keys/
     today). Both projects list "libtam or the program" as an open
     point.

  3. Display (docs/display.md, "Remaining gates and tests"): the
     physical multi-monitor trial and the physical fbcon, VT and desktop
     handoff remain open. They need Fred at the hardware.


WAITING FOR FRED

  - Which of the requests above, if any, comes next.


WATCH OUT

  - Every change bumps VERSION in the same commit: minor for a new
    module or function, patch for a fix. Consumers build against the
    exact libtam they were tested with. A Makefile-only install-location
    change does not bump VERSION: the library itself is unchanged.
  - make test runs under ASan and UBSan; the display suites are make
    test-display test-session.
  - The core must keep needing only libc: anything with another
    dependency goes in its own archive, as the portal and display ones
    do.
  - Never install a development build to /usr/local, and never install
    the display prototype into a live session outside the package Test
    route.
